1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,938
Joseon Korea, the kingdom that prided itself on

2
00:00:02,938 --> 00:00:04,001
Confucian virtue.

3
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,127
But in 1494, a twenty-year-old took the throne—and

4
00:00:07,127 --> 00:00:10,503
within a decade, the royal palace became a place where

5
00:00:10,503 --> 00:00:12,878
scholars bled on the courtyard stones.

6
00:00:12,878 --> 00:00:16,379
This is the story of Yeonsangun, the king who turned his

7
00:00:16,379 --> 00:00:18,317
own court into a killing field.

8
00:00:18,317 --> 00:00:21,506
Born the son of a deposed queen, raised on a secret

9
00:00:21,506 --> 00:00:25,007
grief, he would not stop until the men who destroyed his

10
00:00:25,007 --> 00:00:27,507
mother were dead—and then he kept going.

11
00:00:27,507 --> 00:00:30,758
By 1506, ministers, concubines, and servants had all

12
00:00:30,758 --> 00:00:34,196
learned the same lesson: in Yeonsangun's palace, no one

13
00:00:34,196 --> 00:00:35,088
was safe.

14
00:00:35,638 --> 00:00:39,579
Yeonsangun was born in 1476 to King Seongjong and Queen Yun.

15
00:00:39,579 --> 00:00:43,257
When he was three, his mother was accused of poisoning a

16
00:00:43,257 --> 00:00:45,096
rival concubine and deposed.

17
00:00:45,096 --> 00:00:48,643
Stripped of her title, she was forced to drink poison.

18
00:00:48,643 --> 00:00:52,256
The boy was raised by his stepmother, Queen Jeonghyeon,

19
00:00:52,256 --> 00:00:55,605
told only that his real mother had died of illness.

20
00:00:55,605 --> 00:00:57,247
But the truth leaked out.

21
00:00:57,247 --> 00:00:59,415
A court lady told him everything.

22
00:00:59,415 --> 00:01:02,962
From that day, the young prince carried a private fury

23
00:01:02,962 --> 00:01:07,100
toward the ministers who had signed his mother's death warrant.

24
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:09,202
When Seongjong died in 1494, the

25
00:01:09,202 --> 00:01:12,880
eighteen-year-old—actually twenty by Korean age—ascended

26
00:01:12,880 --> 00:01:16,558
as the tenth king of Joseon, taking the name Yeonsangun.

27
00:01:17,108 --> 00:01:20,750
For a few years, the new king ruled without incident.

28
00:01:20,750 --> 00:01:24,323
Then in 1498, a scholar named Kim Il-son submitted a

29
00:01:24,323 --> 00:01:27,140
memorial criticizing the king's policies.

30
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:29,201
Yeonsangun saw an opportunity.

31
00:01:29,201 --> 00:01:32,431
He ordered an investigation into the records of

32
00:01:32,431 --> 00:01:36,141
Seongjong's reign—and found that several top ministers

33
00:01:36,141 --> 00:01:38,684
had supported Queen Yun's deposition.

34
00:01:38,684 --> 00:01:41,913
The first literati purge, the Muo Sagwa, began.

35
00:01:41,913 --> 00:01:46,105
Kim Il-son and his allies were tortured, exiled, or executed.

36
00:01:46,105 --> 00:01:49,884
The king's message was clear: the men who had destroyed

37
00:01:49,884 --> 00:01:52,014
his mother would answer for it.

38
00:01:52,014 --> 00:01:55,656
But the purge also silenced anyone who dared question

39
00:01:55,656 --> 00:01:56,755
royal authority.

40
00:01:56,755 --> 00:01:58,748
The court learned to whisper.

41
00:01:59,298 --> 00:02:02,570
Six years later, the second purge was far worse.

42
00:02:02,570 --> 00:02:06,047
In 1504, a royal secretary discovered old documents

43
00:02:06,047 --> 00:02:09,387
naming the ministers who had condemned Queen Yun.

44
00:02:09,387 --> 00:02:10,751
Yeonsangun exploded.

45
00:02:10,751 --> 00:02:14,705
The Gapja Sagwa swept through the government like a blade.

46
00:02:14,705 --> 00:02:18,454
Senior officials were beheaded in the palace courtyard.

47
00:02:18,454 --> 00:02:20,431
Others were flogged to death.

48
00:02:20,431 --> 00:02:23,430
The king personally watched some executions.

49
00:02:23,430 --> 00:02:27,112
He ordered that the remains of his mother's enemies be

50
00:02:27,112 --> 00:02:28,611
dug up and desecrated.

51
00:02:28,611 --> 00:02:32,361
Even minor officials who had merely been present at her

52
00:02:32,361 --> 00:02:33,929
trial were hunted down.

53
00:02:33,929 --> 00:02:37,474
By the end, dozens of the kingdom's most experienced

54
00:02:37,474 --> 00:02:41,291
administrators were dead or in exile, and the government

55
00:02:41,291 --> 00:02:42,450
was hollowed out.

56
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:46,635
With his enemies gone, Yeonsangun turned the palace into

57
00:02:46,635 --> 00:02:48,842
his private playground—and prison.

58
00:02:48,842 --> 00:02:52,413
He confiscated hundreds of women from commoner families

59
00:02:52,413 --> 00:02:55,334
to serve as palace entertainers, the gisaeng.

60
00:02:55,334 --> 00:02:58,515
He forced scholars to perform humiliating dances.

61
00:02:58,515 --> 00:03:01,890
He seized homes near the palace for hunting grounds.

62
00:03:01,890 --> 00:03:05,266
When a fire destroyed part of the complex, he had it

63
00:03:05,266 --> 00:03:06,953
rebuilt with forced labor.

64
00:03:06,953 --> 00:03:09,745
Anyone who complained was beaten or killed.

65
00:03:09,745 --> 00:03:12,990
Even concubines who displeased him were strangled.

66
00:03:12,990 --> 00:03:16,626
The king's favorites ruled through terror, and the halls

67
00:03:16,626 --> 00:03:20,715
that once echoed with Confucian debate now echoed with screams.

68
00:03:20,715 --> 00:03:23,896
Joseon's capital had become a theater of cruelty.

69
00:03:24,446 --> 00:03:27,476
By 1506, even the king's allies had had enough.

70
00:03:27,476 --> 00:03:31,022
A coalition of ministers and military commanders—led by

71
00:03:31,022 --> 00:03:34,439
Park Won-jong and Seong Hui-an—moved while Yeonsangun

72
00:03:34,439 --> 00:03:35,536
was away hunting.

73
00:03:35,536 --> 00:03:38,824
They seized the palace, arrested his favorites, and

74
00:03:38,824 --> 00:03:40,178
declared him deposed.

75
00:03:40,178 --> 00:03:43,724
The justification was ancient: a king who had abandoned

76
00:03:43,724 --> 00:03:46,174
virtue had lost the Mandate of Heaven.

77
00:03:46,174 --> 00:03:49,397
Yeonsangun, returning from the hunt, found his own

78
00:03:49,397 --> 00:03:51,138
guards turning against him.

79
00:03:51,138 --> 00:03:54,878
He was stripped of his title and exiled to Ganghwa Island.

80
00:03:54,878 --> 00:03:58,488
The coup was swift and almost bloodless—because by then,

81
00:03:58,488 --> 00:04:01,454
almost no one was left willing to die for him.

82
00:04:02,004 --> 00:04:05,226
Yeonsangun died in exile just two months later, in

83
00:04:05,226 --> 00:04:08,320
November 1506, reportedly of illness—though some

84
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:09,609
whispered of poison.

85
00:04:09,609 --> 00:04:11,156
He was thirty years old.

86
00:04:11,156 --> 00:04:14,572
His half-brother, Jungjong, took the throne and spent

87
00:04:14,572 --> 00:04:16,183
years undoing the damage.

88
00:04:16,183 --> 00:04:19,727
The purges had killed or exiled over a hundred scholars

89
00:04:19,727 --> 00:04:23,788
and officials, gutting Joseon's civil service for a generation.

90
00:04:23,788 --> 00:04:27,397
Historians still debate whether Yeonsangun was a monster

91
00:04:27,397 --> 00:04:30,813
born of grief or a tyrant who simply found an excuse.

92
00:04:30,813 --> 00:04:33,971
What is certain is that the kingdom never forgot.

93
00:04:33,971 --> 00:04:37,193
His reign became a warning: even a throne built on

94
00:04:37,193 --> 00:04:39,900
filial piety can become an altar of blood.

95
00:04:40,450 --> 00:04:43,847
So the next time you walk through the gates of a Joseon

96
00:04:43,847 --> 00:04:46,627
palace, remember what those stones have seen.

97
00:04:46,627 --> 00:04:49,777
Yeonsangun did not destroy his kingdom with foreign

98
00:04:49,777 --> 00:04:53,236
armies—he destroyed it from within, one purge at a time,

99
00:04:53,236 --> 00:04:56,201
until the court itself became the killing field.

100
00:04:56,201 --> 00:04:59,536
History remembers him not as a king, but as a caution.

101
00:04:59,536 --> 00:05:02,748
Subscribe to K-Mystery for more stories from Korea's

102
00:05:02,748 --> 00:05:06,701
shadowed past. And tell us in the comments: was Yeonsangun a son

103
00:05:06,701 --> 00:05:10,654
avenging his mother, or a tyrant who simply enjoyed the killing?

104
00:05:10,654 --> 00:05:12,754
We'll see you in the next mystery.

